  "The innocent reading first. Given a two-word name that is also a term of visualization and portraiture, the Overview defines the term, lists four senses of it and asks which the user means; the paper's artifact is the fourth.",
  ["THE RECORD ONE SENSE OF FOUR. The paper's artifact holds the last bullet and one card of eight. The rest of the field is Du Bois's work for the Paris Exposition ('over 60 hand-drawn charts, graphs, and maps'), Donath's definition, carded from the MIT Media Lab ('Data portraits depict their subjects' accumulated data rather than their faces'), and Lupi's 'wearable buttons at TED 2017', carded twice (giorgialupi, Pentagram).",
   "THE ARTIFACT AT A COARSE GRAIN. 'lightweight inspection tools ... that record dataset training traces.' The abstract: 'artifacts that record training data and allow for downstream inspection' and 'Our tool is lightweight and fast, costing only 3% of the dataset size in overhead.' The recording and the lightness are carried; 'training traces' is the composition's phrase. The question the artifact answers ('has the model already encountered a given example during training?'), its method ('a solution based on data sketching'), the two corpora it documents ('The Pile' and 'The Stack') and its uses ('test set leakage and model plagiarism') are absent.",
   "NO AUTHOR, NO VENUE. 'Contemporary research groups use the term': the abstract's authors, 'Marc Marone , Benjamin Van Durme', and its venue, 'NeurIPS 2023 Datasets and Benchmarks', are not named, and the sense is undated. Each of the other three senses carries a person ('Sociologist W. E. B. Du Bois', 'Digital media scholar Judith Donath', 'Designers like Giorgia Lupi').",
   "A PAGE UNDER THE NAME INDEXED. The second card, titled 'Data Portraits' under the site name 'Data Portraits', carries the abstract's opening ('Foundation models are trained on increasingly immense and opaque datasets. Even while these models are now key in AI system building, it can be difficult to ans...'), and the composition's one link on the artifact goes to it. The census row records the 'live interface' as released and unread, and its lifecycle leaves open 'whether the live interface is still served'; on 2026-11-01 a page under the name is in the index, at an address the paste does not show."],
  "A disambiguation in which the training-data record is the last of four senses, named by the term alone with no author, venue, corpus or question. The membership query the census row reads it for, whether a work is in a recorded corpus, is not carried.",
  [C("giorgialupi", "Data Portraits at TED — giorgialupi", "Project: Data Portraits at TED 2017 (Vancouver) by Accurat, commissioned by TED and David Stark Design for Target's social space. Concept: Personal questionnair..."),
   C("Data Portraits", "Data Portraits", "Foundation models are trained on increasingly immense and opaque datasets. Even while these models are now key in AI system building, it can be difficult to ans...", note="a page under the artifact's name, carrying the paper's abstract"),
   C("Zinn Education Project", "W. E. B. Du Bois's Data Portraits: Visualizing Black America - Zinn Education Project", "Book: W. E. B. Du Bois's Data Portraits: Visualizing Black America (2018, 144 pages, Princeton Architectural Press). Creators: W. E. B. Du Bois Center at Univer..."),
   C("Pentagram", "TED Data Portraits - Pentagram", "Recognizing what they had in common at a glance, attendees found it easy to start. Recognizing what they had in common at a glance, attendees found it easy to s..."),
   C("SparkMap", "W.E.B. Du Bois & Data Visualization History - SparkMap", "Sociologist W.E.B. Du Bois created sixty hand-drawn \"data portraits\" between 1897 and 1990 with Atlanta University students and scholars, visualizing Black Amer..."),
   C("YouTube·University of Massachusetts Amherst Libraries", "W. E. B. Du Bois's Data Portraits: Visualizing Black America", None, note="video card, 5m"),
   C("MIT Media Lab", "Data Portraits - MIT Media Lab", "Data portraits depict their subjects' accumulated data rather than their faces. They can be visualizations of discussion contributions, browsing histories, soci..."),
   C("ARTnews.com", "10 Painstakingly Crafted Data Portraits That Reveal Strange Details of People's ...", "Your tweets, your Instagrams, your Facebook status updates, your search history—it's all a part of your data portrait. And right now, by reading this article, y...")],
